    Success removes the item, persists, broadcasts, and marks the user
    message sent for notifications. Failure keeps the item, backs off
    2 s → 60 s (doubling per consecutive failure of that item), and re-arms.
+   Each queued item maps to a stable OpenCode message id (`msg_` + the queue
+   item id). A same-id repost is not ignored by OpenCode — it appends the
+   parts again — so a timed-out send must be told apart from a missing one:
+   before every (re)send and right after a failure the runtime probes
+   `GET /session/:id/message/:messageId`. A found message means the ack was
+   lost — the item is removed instead of delivered twice. A probe that fails
+   is unknown, never "missing": the tick re-arms without sending until the
+   message is confirmed absent. One absent probe right after a failed send is
+   not yet proof — the first attempt may still be running before it persists —
+   so a resend also requires the absence to hold across a second probe spaced
+   by the retry backoff.
 6. The next item goes out after the next busy → idle cycle.
 
